0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"The Nilo-Saharan languages are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and Nile rivers, including historic Nubia, north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet.",Lokoya,Nilo-Saharan
1,Lokoya | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/lky/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..V3ELN-7zN1rmFfk9HzPOvDgFCW0KJsyix1OR_ns9COE,Lokoya is an endangered indigenous language of South Sudan. It belongs to the Nilo-Saharan language family.,Lokoya,Nilo-Saharan
2,"Lokoya alphabet, prounciation and language",https://www.omniglot.com/writing/lokoya.htm,"Lokoya is a Nilo-Saharan language spoken to the east of Juba in South Sudan by about 12,400 people.",Lokoya,Nilo-Saharan
3,Lokoya language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/lokoya_language,"Lokoya belongs to the Nilo-Saharan language family, positioned within the Eastern Sudanic branch as part of the Nilotic languages, specifically the Eastern Nilotic subdivision.",Lokoya,Nilo-Saharan
